1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,033
Eleven at night

2
00:00:01,033 --> 00:00:02,800
You open the job app again

3
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,033
Scroll through a few listings

4
00:00:04,033 --> 00:00:05,600
None of them feel like you

5
00:00:05,766 --> 00:00:07,466
You switch to a course page

6
00:00:07,566 --> 00:00:09,200
Photography looks good too

7
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:11,166
Coding seems to have a future too

8
00:00:11,366 --> 00:00:13,233
Making content sounds free

9
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:15,600
Learning a craft feels more solid

10
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,033
After all that scrolling

11
00:00:17,033 --> 00:00:18,600
You end up clicking nothing

12
00:00:18,666 --> 00:00:20,066
Phone face down by the pillow

13
00:00:20,066 --> 00:00:21,466
One thought left in your head

14
00:00:21,466 --> 00:00:23,366
What do I actually like

15
00:00:23,766 --> 00:00:25,700
That question traps a lot of people

16
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:28,366
It sounds like you're searching for direction

17
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:31,166
But sometimes what it's really asking is

18
00:00:31,366 --> 00:00:33,033
Before I even put in the effort

19
00:00:33,033 --> 00:00:35,500
Just tell me which road won't be wasted

20
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:37,966
We're so used to imagining passion

21
00:00:37,966 --> 00:00:39,766
as an answer that's hidden somewhere

22
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:42,666
As if everyone has a lamp inside them

23
00:00:42,966 --> 00:00:44,233
And once you find the right switch

24
00:00:44,233 --> 00:00:45,800
It just lights up

25
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:48,433
So some people keep taking personality tests

26
00:00:48,666 --> 00:00:51,500
Some study every little interest so carefully

27
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:55,100
Some wait for something to spark a rush of excitement

28
00:00:55,700 --> 00:00:57,666
No spark no starting

29
00:00:57,966 --> 00:01:01,000
But that's rarely how passion actually shows up

30
00:01:01,066 --> 00:01:02,800
The coffee you love drinking now

31
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,200
Probably didn't taste special the first time

32
00:01:05,366 --> 00:01:07,366
The work you handle so smoothly now

33
00:01:07,433 --> 00:01:09,266
It probably started out as

34
00:01:09,266 --> 00:01:10,900
clumsy little tasks

35
00:01:11,500 --> 00:01:13,166
That sport you fell for

36
00:01:13,166 --> 00:01:15,166
Probably wasn't fun the first time

37
00:01:15,366 --> 00:01:17,433
It was after learning a bit

38
00:01:17,666 --> 00:01:19,566
that you finally felt in control

39
00:01:20,566 --> 00:01:22,700
A lot of what you love isn't discovered

40
00:01:22,700 --> 00:01:25,833
It grows slowly after you've done it a few times

41
00:01:26,033 --> 00:01:29,166
That's not great news if you're in a hurry for an answer

42
00:01:29,166 --> 00:01:31,033
Because it means you have to

43
00:01:31,033 --> 00:01:32,700
let yourself stay unsure first

44
00:01:33,366 --> 00:01:36,433
You have to try things that don't look worth it yet

45
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:39,400
You have to accept the first few tries won't be good

46
00:01:40,066 --> 00:01:41,066
But think about it

47
00:01:41,066 --> 00:01:42,366
What really traps people

48
00:01:42,366 --> 00:01:43,866
usually isn't having no options

49
00:01:44,166 --> 00:01:45,833
It's after having too many

50
00:01:45,833 --> 00:01:48,400
when every one feels like a test you can't fail

51
00:01:48,633 --> 00:01:49,866
You're afraid learning it won't pay off

52
00:01:49,866 --> 00:01:51,400
Afraid switching paths will make people

53
00:01:51,400 --> 00:01:53,066
see you as unstable

54
00:01:53,566 --> 00:01:55,033
Afraid that after three months

55
00:01:55,033 --> 00:01:56,466
you'll find out you don't like it

56
00:01:56,766 --> 00:02:00,100
So the safest move seems to be just keep thinking

57
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:02,766
But endless thinking has a cost too

58
00:02:02,766 --> 00:02:05,433
You get better and better at judging other people's paths

59
00:02:05,433 --> 00:02:07,633
And end up with less and less proof of your own

60
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:10,766
This is exactly where passion gets misunderstood

61
00:02:10,966 --> 00:02:13,233
It's not a pass you're handed before you set off

62
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:14,966
It's often only after you've walked a little way

63
00:02:14,966 --> 00:02:16,600
that you're handed the receipt

64
00:02:18,033 --> 00:02:20,566
Economics has a really useful distinction

65
00:02:20,566 --> 00:02:22,266
Called exploration and exploitation

66
00:02:22,566 --> 00:02:23,766
Exploitation is easy to get

67
00:02:23,866 --> 00:02:26,366
You already know which restaurant is good

68
00:02:26,366 --> 00:02:28,566
So every time you're hungry you go back there

69
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:29,633
It saves time

70
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:30,833
And you rarely get burned

71
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:33,366
Exploration is occasionally trying

72
00:02:33,366 --> 00:02:35,033
some place you've never been

73
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:36,100
You might get burned

74
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:38,266
Or you might find a taste you love even more

75
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:39,866
If all you do is exploit

76
00:02:39,866 --> 00:02:41,266
Life stays pretty stable

77
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,566
But your choices only ever come from

78
00:02:43,566 --> 00:02:45,800
that small slice you already know

79
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:47,466
If all you do is explore

80
00:02:47,466 --> 00:02:49,200
You keep switching from one thing to another

81
00:02:49,266 --> 00:02:51,700
And it's hard to go deep on anything

82
00:02:51,766 --> 00:02:53,366
Finding what you love works the same way

83
00:02:53,566 --> 00:02:56,000
A lot of people want to jump straight to exploiting

84
00:02:57,066 --> 00:02:59,700
They want to find the perfect career immediately

85
00:02:59,766 --> 00:03:03,500
With just the right pay growth freedom and meaning

86
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:04,500
But here's the problem

87
00:03:04,500 --> 00:03:06,166
You haven't explored anything yet

88
00:03:06,233 --> 00:03:08,466
You don't even have enough samples to work with

89
00:03:09,433 --> 00:03:11,500
You're letting other people's videos

90
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:13,033
other people's resumes and

91
00:03:13,033 --> 00:03:14,866
other people's feelings decide for you

92
00:03:15,100 --> 00:03:17,366
It's like never having tasted a dish

93
00:03:17,366 --> 00:03:20,300
And judging whether you'll like it just from the reviews

94
00:03:21,200 --> 00:03:22,033
It's hard

95
00:03:22,666 --> 00:03:24,833
Because liking something

96
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:26,666
rarely comes from its name

97
00:03:26,833 --> 00:03:29,100
It comes from what it feels like to do it

98
00:03:29,566 --> 00:03:32,166
When you write do you hate researching

99
00:03:32,166 --> 00:03:33,600
or hate revising sentences

100
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:36,366
When you shoot video do you love hunting for footage

101
00:03:36,366 --> 00:03:38,200
or explaining something clearly

102
00:03:38,433 --> 00:03:41,066
When you work with data do you enjoy finding patterns

103
00:03:41,066 --> 00:03:43,100
or making tidy spreadsheets

104
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:45,200
None of these details show up

105
00:03:45,200 --> 00:03:46,466
until you actually do it once

106
00:03:46,766 --> 00:03:48,900
There's something else people often overlook

107
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:50,066
It's called a sense of competence

108
00:03:50,666 --> 00:03:53,566
The first time you do most things there's no passion at all

109
00:03:53,566 --> 00:03:55,300
Because all you feel is clumsy

110
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,200
Your fingers hurt pressing guitar strings

111
00:03:57,200 --> 00:03:59,033
You can never find the rhythm editing video

112
00:03:59,166 --> 00:04:00,966
You put too much salt in the food

113
00:04:01,166 --> 00:04:04,033
You stare at the first article unable to write an opening

114
00:04:04,033 --> 00:04:06,800
If all you ask yourself then is whether you like it

115
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:08,566
The answer is usually no

116
00:04:09,033 --> 00:04:10,266
But once you do it a few more times

117
00:04:10,266 --> 00:04:11,666
Your hands start to cooperate

118
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:13,266
You can see a little progress

119
00:04:13,300 --> 00:04:14,666
Only then are you qualified to know

120
00:04:14,666 --> 00:04:16,433
if you're actually interested

121
00:04:16,666 --> 00:04:19,200
Not all persistence grows into passion

122
00:04:19,200 --> 00:04:20,600
But without any repetition

123
00:04:20,666 --> 00:04:22,433
passion rarely grows at all

124
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:24,766
Passion isn't talent speaking up

125
00:04:24,833 --> 00:04:26,433
Most of the time it's feedback

126
00:04:26,433 --> 00:04:28,166
finally letting you hear yourself

127
00:04:29,100 --> 00:04:31,300
Picture a scene you know all too well

128
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:32,566
A class reunion

129
00:04:32,566 --> 00:04:33,833
Someone landed a job at a big company

130
00:04:33,833 --> 00:04:35,233
Someone got a stable government job

131
00:04:35,266 --> 00:04:36,666
Someone started their own business

132
00:04:37,100 --> 00:04:40,233
Listening to them everyone seems to have found their path

133
00:04:40,433 --> 00:04:41,400
When it's your turn to talk

134
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:43,066
Suddenly any direction that's still

135
00:04:43,066 --> 00:04:45,266
uncertain feels embarrassing

136
00:04:45,900 --> 00:04:48,366
So you don't dare say you just want to try something

137
00:04:48,433 --> 00:04:51,000
You're afraid someone will ask if this can make money

138
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,066
How long can you keep doing it

139
00:04:52,166 --> 00:04:53,766
What if it fails

140
00:04:53,900 --> 00:04:55,866
There's nothing wrong with these questions themselves

141
00:04:55,900 --> 00:04:56,900
The problem is

142
00:04:57,000 --> 00:05:00,200
We keep asking them way too early

143
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:03,100
You haven't even finished writing one full piece

144
00:05:03,166 --> 00:05:04,500
And you're already forcing yourself to answer

145
00:05:04,500 --> 00:05:06,700
if writing can pay the bills

146
00:05:07,033 --> 00:05:09,166
You haven't taken a single pottery class

147
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:11,366
And you're already calculating the payoff three years out

148
00:05:11,833 --> 00:05:13,500
You've never led a single project

149
00:05:13,500 --> 00:05:16,266
And you're already deciding if you're cut out for management

150
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:17,766
That's not caution

151
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:21,200
That's using the final question to block the very first step

152
00:05:21,766 --> 00:05:23,033
There's another pressure too

153
00:05:23,033 --> 00:05:25,166
from all those picture-perfect lives online

154
00:05:25,400 --> 00:05:27,966
Someone knew they'd start a business at twenty

155
00:05:28,066 --> 00:05:30,500
Someone switched careers and got results right away

156
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,400
Someone turned a hobby into a beautiful little channel

157
00:05:33,633 --> 00:05:36,300
What you see is the highlight reel they edited

158
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:38,700
Not the boring trial and error behind it

159
00:05:39,566 --> 00:05:41,633
You start to assume that people with real direction

160
00:05:41,633 --> 00:05:43,633
never hesitated from the start

161
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:46,100
Actually most of them didn't start with a direction

162
00:05:46,266 --> 00:05:49,366
They started with something so small nobody noticed

163
00:05:49,466 --> 00:05:51,900
And the direction only surfaced as they kept going

164
00:05:52,100 --> 00:05:54,233
The third trap is thinking passion

165
00:05:54,866 --> 00:05:56,433
means being happy forever

166
00:05:56,800 --> 00:05:57,866
You love drawing

167
00:05:57,866 --> 00:05:59,766
doesn't mean you'll love endless revisions

168
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:01,066
You love making content

169
00:06:01,066 --> 00:06:03,833
doesn't mean you'll love the days the numbers tank

170
00:06:03,966 --> 00:06:05,066
You love teaching

171
00:06:05,066 --> 00:06:07,366
doesn't mean you'll love prepping lessons till midnight

172
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:09,300
A thing worth sticking with

173
00:06:09,466 --> 00:06:11,266
isn't one that never tires you out

174
00:06:11,633 --> 00:06:13,966
it's one you're still willing to come back to after it does

175
00:06:14,066 --> 00:06:16,433
That's a far more reliable test

176
00:06:16,700 --> 00:06:18,866
Not asking if you're excited the whole time

177
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:20,633
But asking if you're willing to make

178
00:06:20,633 --> 00:06:22,366
this thing just a little better

179
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:26,800
So don't treat one attempt like a life sentence

180
00:06:27,033 --> 00:06:29,066
It's more like trying on a pair of shoes

181
00:06:29,066 --> 00:06:30,566
You have to walk a few steps in them first

182
00:06:30,566 --> 00:06:32,000
Before you know where they rub

183
00:06:32,200 --> 00:06:34,066
And where they can actually take you far

184
00:06:35,033 --> 00:06:37,800
If you still don't know what you like right now

185
00:06:38,066 --> 00:06:40,900
Stop forcing yourself to answer the huge question

186
00:06:41,100 --> 00:06:43,433
Swap it for a much smaller one

187
00:06:43,633 --> 00:06:45,866
Am I willing to give this two hours

188
00:06:45,900 --> 00:06:46,900
Not three years

189
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:47,966
Not a lifetime

190
00:06:47,966 --> 00:06:49,200
Just two hours

191
00:06:50,066 --> 00:06:51,066
Want to try writing

192
00:06:51,066 --> 00:06:53,700
Find a question that's genuinely been bugging you lately

193
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:55,566
Write five hundred words and show a friend

194
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:56,700
Want to try making video

195
00:06:56,700 --> 00:06:59,600
Grab your phone and explain something small you know well

196
00:07:00,066 --> 00:07:01,033
Want to try design

197
00:07:01,033 --> 00:07:02,300
Redesign one screen

198
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:04,400
of an app you use all the time

199
00:07:04,633 --> 00:07:05,566
Want to try cooking

200
00:07:05,566 --> 00:07:08,033
Carefully recreate a dish you love eating

201
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:10,366
This little experiment needs three limits

202
00:07:10,600 --> 00:07:12,866
First don't spend too much money

203
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:15,800
Don't buy a room full of gear the moment you're mildly curious

204
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:18,800
Second don't let it eat all your time

205
00:07:18,866 --> 00:07:20,700
Don't demand of yourself from day one

206
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:22,766
that you do it every single night after work

207
00:07:23,266 --> 00:07:26,300
Third leave behind something you can actually see

208
00:07:26,466 --> 00:07:27,600
An article

209
00:07:27,633 --> 00:07:30,833
A piece of audio a dish a drawing

210
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:33,366
Or an interview you actually carried out

211
00:07:33,866 --> 00:07:35,666
It doesn't have to go online

212
00:07:35,766 --> 00:07:37,433
It's evidence for yourself

213
00:07:38,233 --> 00:07:39,000
When you're done

214
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:40,800
don't just ask if you liked it

215
00:07:40,900 --> 00:07:42,566
You can write down three things

216
00:07:42,700 --> 00:07:44,633
How much I resisted before starting

217
00:07:44,833 --> 00:07:47,600
Which part I was most absorbed in while doing it

218
00:07:47,833 --> 00:07:50,433
Whether I'd be willing to do it again afterward

219
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:52,100
And a fourth question

220
00:07:52,566 --> 00:07:53,566
If I did it again

221
00:07:53,566 --> 00:07:55,833
which step would I want to do a little better

222
00:07:56,166 --> 00:07:57,566
That question matters a lot

223
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,600
Because it helps you tell two kinds of tired apart

224
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:02,300
One kind is after finishing something

225
00:08:02,300 --> 00:08:04,000
you never want to touch it again

226
00:08:04,233 --> 00:08:06,800
The other is feeling clumsy

227
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,400
but already thinking how to improve next time

228
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:11,600
That second kind is usually

229
00:08:11,600 --> 00:08:13,366
more reliable than a brief burst of excitement

230
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:15,066
It might not feel like passion

231
00:08:15,066 --> 00:08:16,766
It's more like a willingness to lean in

232
00:08:17,266 --> 00:08:20,233
You don't have to give it some grand name right away

233
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,466
No need to ask if it's your calling

234
00:08:22,833 --> 00:08:25,100
No rush to turn it into a side hustle either

235
00:08:25,433 --> 00:08:28,800
Just let it be one small thing done properly for now

236
00:08:29,033 --> 00:08:30,833
I have a friend who likes photography

237
00:08:30,966 --> 00:08:33,366
At first she always felt she had no eye for it

238
00:08:33,566 --> 00:08:35,500
She didn't sign up for some expensive class right away

239
00:08:36,300 --> 00:08:39,100
She just spent four weekends straight going to

240
00:08:39,100 --> 00:08:41,100
the same block shooting for an hour

241
00:08:41,500 --> 00:08:43,200
Week one she shot signs

242
00:08:43,366 --> 00:08:44,866
Week two windows

243
00:08:45,166 --> 00:08:47,266
Week three only strangers' hands

244
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:50,033
Week four she went home and laid all the photos out together

245
00:08:51,166 --> 00:08:53,433
She still wouldn't say she's passionate about photography

246
00:08:53,433 --> 00:08:54,900
But she already knows

247
00:08:54,900 --> 00:08:56,700
she's willing to get up early for the light

248
00:08:56,700 --> 00:08:59,566
And willing to stare at one photo a few extra minutes

249
00:08:59,666 --> 00:09:01,100
That's the evidence

250
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:04,300
Not someone praising your talent

251
00:09:04,600 --> 00:09:06,400
It's noticing you keep

252
00:09:06,400 --> 00:09:08,366
bringing your own attention back here

253
00:09:08,833 --> 00:09:09,366
Or say

254
00:09:09,366 --> 00:09:12,000
someone wants to know if they're cut out for making content

255
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:13,866
Don't stare at the view count first

256
00:09:13,866 --> 00:09:16,166
First try clearly explaining three

257
00:09:16,166 --> 00:09:18,100
questions you actually care about

258
00:09:18,366 --> 00:09:19,800
After you're done check

259
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,366
Do you only care about the numbers

260
00:09:21,400 --> 00:09:23,166
Or does it feel good that someone

261
00:09:23,166 --> 00:09:25,233
finally got it

262
00:09:25,666 --> 00:09:28,500
Different feedback pulls you in different directions

263
00:09:28,966 --> 00:09:30,366
You're not running this

264
00:09:30,366 --> 00:09:32,433
experiment to prove you're impressive

265
00:09:32,566 --> 00:09:35,400
You're using it to clear up how well you actually know yourself

266
00:09:36,366 --> 00:09:38,800
Of course these small experiments need some repetition too

267
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:40,766
Try writing something today

268
00:09:40,900 --> 00:09:42,700
Try five minutes of piano tomorrow

269
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:44,633
Switch to baking the day after

270
00:09:44,866 --> 00:09:47,633
Hopping around like that won't give you a real read

271
00:09:47,766 --> 00:09:50,266
The best approach is to pick one direction

272
00:09:50,766 --> 00:09:53,066
Give it three to five real tries

273
00:09:53,233 --> 00:09:55,100
Changing just one small thing each time

274
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:57,166
Then decide whether to keep going

275
00:09:57,400 --> 00:09:59,066
That's not delaying a decision

276
00:09:59,066 --> 00:10:00,466
It's trading a smaller cost

277
00:10:00,466 --> 00:10:02,433
for a truer answer

278
00:10:02,633 --> 00:10:05,600
That gets you closer to yourself than any personality test

279
00:10:05,700 --> 00:10:08,000
Because it's not the version of you that you imagine

280
00:10:08,433 --> 00:10:11,166
It's the reaction that's left after you actually did it

281
00:10:11,366 --> 00:10:14,100
Some experiments will prove you genuinely don't like something

282
00:10:14,100 --> 00:10:15,366
That's not wasted either

283
00:10:15,633 --> 00:10:17,200
Ruling out a path that doesn't fit

284
00:10:17,200 --> 00:10:18,966
is a win for exploration too

285
00:10:19,233 --> 00:10:22,166
Some experiments won't get your blood pumping right away

286
00:10:22,300 --> 00:10:23,566
But you'll notice you're

287
00:10:23,566 --> 00:10:25,033
still willing to give it a bit more time

288
00:10:25,033 --> 00:10:26,066
And that's enough

289
00:10:26,466 --> 00:10:27,833
Passion doesn't have to

290
00:10:27,833 --> 00:10:29,466
strike like lightning in a movie right away

291
00:10:29,666 --> 00:10:32,300
It can start like a small flame just lit on the stove

292
00:10:32,466 --> 00:10:34,800
Small at first but you keep coming back to check on it

293
00:10:34,900 --> 00:10:37,166
You don't have to find your life's answer right now

294
00:10:37,166 --> 00:10:38,600
You just need to stop waiting for

295
00:10:38,600 --> 00:10:40,233
an answer that requires no starting

296
00:10:40,866 --> 00:10:43,700
Next time you scroll past someone's shiny career story

297
00:10:44,200 --> 00:10:46,833
Don't rush to ask if you should be doing that too

298
00:10:46,900 --> 00:10:49,666
Ask yourself if you can give something a try this week

299
00:10:49,900 --> 00:10:51,700
Passion isn't something you find

300
00:10:51,800 --> 00:10:54,466
It's doing it again and again then revising

301
00:10:54,600 --> 00:10:56,600
and slowly recognizing your own shape
